# Terminal operations

The op that **runs** the recipe — produces a result that is *not* a stream. Exactly one ends every pipeline.

## Eager, and one-shot

Calling it executes the whole pipeline, then **consumes** the stream:

```java
long n = s.count();     // runs it
s.forEach(...);         // IllegalStateException — s is spent
```

## Reduce to one value

```java
stream.count();          // long
intStream.sum();         // 5050 (primitive streams)
stream.min(cmp);         // Optional<T> — might be empty
stream.max(cmp);
```

## Search — short-circuits

```java
stream.anyMatch(p);      // true the instant one passes
stream.allMatch(p);
stream.findFirst();      // Optional<T>, then stop
```

## Collect / side-effect

```java
List<String> list = stream.toList();      // Java 16+
stream.forEach(System.out::println);      // void — real side effects only
```

**Why `Optional`, not `null`?** An empty stream has no first / min / max — the type makes you handle it. → §09.
